  "text_md": "These values of coeﬃcients are enough to reconstruct the full dependence:\n\nAa,b,c = 2(a + b + c) + 3 Ca,b|c,d = 3(2a + 1)(2b + 1) Ga = (2a + 1)(2a2 + 2a + 1)\n\nNow we are able to write the full Hamiltonian for SIB polynomials:\n\n(132)\n\n∞\n\n∂3 ∂τa∂τb∂τc\n\nHˆ =\n\n(2(a + b + c) + 3)τa+b+c+1\n\n+\n\n![](<2503.07583_pg22_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\na,b,c=0\n\n∞\n\n∂ ∂τa+b+c+1\n\n4(2a + 1)(2b + 1)(2c + 1)τaτbτc\n\n+\n\n![](<2503.07583_pg22_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\na,b,c=0\n\n∂2 ∂τc∂τd\n\n3(2a + 1)(2b + 1)τaτb\n\n![](<2503.07583_pg22_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\na+b=c+d\n\n∞\n\n∂ ∂τa\n\n(2a + 1) 2a2 + 2a + 1 τa\n\n![](<2503.07583_pg22_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\na=0\n\n(133)\n\nOne can check that eigenvalues of the above operator are positive integers and obey formula (130). This Hamiltonian itself does not deﬁne SIB basis due to degenerate spectrum, for example ε[8,7] = 83 + 73 = 855 = 93 + 53 + 13 = ε[9,5,1]. However, with adding/removing rules the basis is ﬁxed completely.\n\n# 3.5 Action of Virasoro operators on SIBλ polynomials\n\nMotivated by the action of time-variables and Virasoro operators on SIB polynomials that we discuss in this section it is instructive to slightly change notation of diagrams DP - distinct partitions. For this purpose we represent a Young diagram (turned upside down) as a way of tight packing of square boxes in a corner of a room. In this setting distinct partitions (DP) are in one-to-one correspondence with possible ways of tight packing of square boxes under a ladder. We demonstrate examples of these diagrams, left picture correspond to usual Young diagram, right one correspond to distinct partition:\n\n(a) Usual partition (a) Distinct partition\n\n(134) Form now on we represent distinct partitions as Young diagrams under the ladder. With these\n\nnotations the action of τ0 and ∂τ∂\n\nhave the following from: τ0 · SIBλ =\n\n![](<2503.07583_pg22_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\n0\n\nSIBλ+ (135)\n\n∈AddDP(λ)\n\n22\n\n",
